WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:07.003
A drug that fills nine in ten of its targets looks nearly done.

2
00:00:07.923 --> 00:00:08.923
Is it?

3
00:00:09.317 --> 00:00:13.717
Take paroxetine. It blocks the transporter that clears serotonin.

4
00:00:14.867 --> 00:00:17.387
Picture the transporters as a field of dots.

5
00:00:18.247 --> 00:00:20.907
As the drug's concentration rises, the dots fill.

6
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.920
Half fill at a very low concentration.

7
00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.160
That concentration is the dissociation constant, KD.

8
00:00:29.183 --> 00:00:31.983
Nine in ten takes nine times that concentration.

9
00:00:33.167 --> 00:00:37.947
Ninety-nine in a hundred takes eleven times the 90% % concentration.

10
00:00:39.117 --> 00:00:43.197
The curve flattens at the top, so the last few sites cost far more drug.

11
00:00:44.167 --> 00:00:46.067
Do those last few sites matter?

12
00:00:47.107 --> 00:00:48.667
Build a simple model to find out.

13
00:00:49.567 --> 00:00:53.447
Suppose the transporter is the only way out, and is far from busy.

14
00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:56.907
Block half, and serotonin lingers twice as long.

15
00:00:57.867 --> 00:01:01.127
Block nine in ten, and it lingers ten times as long.

16
00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:06.630
Block ninety-nine in a hundred, and it lingers about a hundred times as long.

17
00:01:07.583 --> 00:01:10.363
That stretch is the factor 1 + L/K.

18
00:01:11.283 --> 00:01:12.783
L is the free drug concentration.

19
00:01:13.843 --> 00:01:15.763
K is the drug's half-fill concentration.

20
00:01:16.917 --> 00:01:18.917
Real tissue has other routes out.

21
00:01:19.777 --> 00:01:23.857
They make all three numbers smaller, and limit the stretch most when the block is

22
00:01:23.857 --> 00:01:24.857
nearly complete.

23
00:01:25.617 --> 00:01:27.697
The same factor appears at a receptor.

24
00:01:28.937 --> 00:01:30.457
Picture GABA's dose-response curve.

25
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:34.320
A competitive antagonist slides the curve right.

26
00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:38.660
You need more GABA, by that same factor, but you still reach the top.

27
00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:44.710
Diazepam, a modulator at a separate site, slides the curve to the left instead.

28
00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:47.310
Keep adding bicuculline.

29
00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:50.510
Then, separately, keep adding diazepam.

30
00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:52.190
What happens to each curve?

31
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.200
The antagonist keeps pushing the curve right, for as long as you add more.

32
00:02:03.183 --> 00:02:04.483
The modulator stops.

33
00:02:05.443 --> 00:02:07.763
Once its own sites are full, it can do no more.

34
00:02:08.583 --> 00:02:09.583
That is its ceiling.

35
00:02:10.433 --> 00:02:15.493
One warning. This rule is for blockers that compete, not for agonists.

36
00:02:16.433 --> 00:02:17.973
An agonist is different.

37
00:02:18.833 --> 00:02:22.933
With spare receptors, filling a tenth of them can give about half the full effect.

38
00:02:23.967 --> 00:02:28.927
The takeaway: if the transporter is the only way out, the last few percent blocked

39
00:02:28.927 --> 00:02:30.127
stretch the signal most.
